            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "mgl_contact_disks draws a flat disk on the floor at each place a body is touching the ground, with the disk's area standing for the force there.\n\nWeight moving from one foot to the other becomes something you watch rather than read.\n\nTHE DATA:\n'contacts' takes one row per contact: x, y, z and a force - what smpl_torque's contact_points outlet sends, with the force in newtons. A flat list is read four numbers at a time, and an empty array clears the disks. A contact with no force (or a negative one) is not drawn.\n\nTHE SIZE:\nThe force times 'area_scale' is the disk's AREA, so the radius grows as the square root of the force - twice the force is twice the area, not twice the width. With the default 0.001, 500 newtons (a foot carrying most of a person's weight) is a disk of radius about 0.4. 'min_radius' and 'max_radius' clamp the radius; a max_radius of 0 or less means no limit.\n\nTHE DISKS:\nEach disk lies flat in the x-z plane - y is up - at the contact's height plus 'height_offset', which lifts it just clear of a floor drawn at the same height. For z-up data, turn the chain before this node. The disks are lit shapes in the current colour, so they need an mgl_light earlier in the chain, and they take the usual shape inputs: 'mode' (solid, wireframe or points), 'cull', 'point_size', 'round' and 'texture'. 'segments' sets how round each disk is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_contact_disks\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_contact_disks\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. 'draw' draws the disks.\n\ncontacts:\nx y z force, one row per contact. Kept until new contacts arrive.\n\narea_scale:\nDisk area per unit of force. 0.001 by default.\n\nmin_radius / max_radius:\nLimits on the radius. 0 and 0.5 by default.\n\nheight_offset:\nLift above the contact height. 0.001 by default.\n\nsegments:\nRoundness. 24 by default.\n\nmode / cull / point_size / round / texture:\nAs for the other mgl shapes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, continuing.\n\nRELATED:\nsmpl_torque produces the contacts. mgl_body draws the body standing on them; mgl_torque_arc draws the joint torques.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 800,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 800,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "mgl_contact_disks draws a flat disk on the floor at each place a body is touching the ground, with the disk's area standing for the force there.\n\nWeight moving from one foot to the other becomes something you watch rather than read.\n\nTHE DATA:\n'contacts' takes one row per contact: x, y, z and a force - what smpl_torque's contact_points outlet sends, with the force in newtons. A flat list is read four numbers at a time, and an empty array clears the disks. A contact with no force (or a negative one) is not drawn.\n\nTHE SIZE:\nThe force times 'area_scale' is the disk's AREA, so the radius grows as the square root of the force - twice the force is twice the area, not twice the width. With the default 0.001, 500 newtons (a foot carrying most of a person's weight) is a disk of radius about 0.4. 'min_radius' and 'max_radius' clamp the radius; a max_radius of 0 or less means no limit.\n\nTHE DISKS:\nEach disk lies flat in the x-z plane - y is up - at the contact's height plus 'height_offset', which lifts it just clear of a floor drawn at the same height. For z-up data, turn the chain before this node. The disks are lit shapes in the current colour, so they need an mgl_light earlier in the chain, and they take the usual shape inputs: 'mode' (solid, wireframe or points), 'cull', 'point_size', 'round' and 'texture'. 'segments' sets how round each disk is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_contact_disks\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_contact_disks\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. 'draw' draws the disks.\n\ncontacts:\nx y z force, one row per contact. Kept until new contacts arrive.\n\narea_scale:\nDisk area per unit of force. 0.001 by default.\n\nmin_radius / max_radius:\nLimits on the radius. 0 and 0.5 by default.\n\nheight_offset:\nLift above the contact height. 0.001 by default.\n\nsegments:\nRoundness. 24 by default.\n\nmode / cull / point_size / round / texture:\nAs for the other mgl shapes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, continuing.\n\nRELATED:\nsmpl_torque produces the contacts. mgl_body draws the body standing on them; mgl_torque_arc draws the joint torques."
        },
